    {
      "id": "concept-private-ipv4-local-network-horror",
      "kind": "concept",
      "promptEs": "asesino · bloque 192.168/16 · red local · IPv4",
      "questionEn": "Why does the network prefix turn the campfire story into a technical horror joke?",
      "answerEn": "192.168.0.0/16 is private IPv4 address space, so an address in that block suggests that the killer is on the same local network rather than somewhere remote.",
      "noteEn": "The abbreviated prefix 192.168/16 denotes the private-use range from 192.168.0.0 through 192.168.255.255 reserved by RFC 1918. The listener's alarm treats local-network proximity like the classic horror reveal that danger is already inside. The title text adds a second programmer joke: a century later, continuing to use IPv4 would itself be frighteningly outdated.",
      "tags": [
        "concept",
        "IPv4",
        "private network",
        "RFC 1918",
        "horror joke"
      ],
      "reviewStatus": "ai-authored-internal-qa",
